HOWTO
=====

Files:

        raytraceN.map   map file for N processors
        raytraceN.btl   boot file for N processors
        testall.sh      run every arrangement and check the rendered image
        multi.sh        run one arrangement interactively

Quick check that everything works:

        ./testall.sh --max 8          # shared-memory links
        ./testall.sh --ipc            # every arrangement over ipc:// (use this at N>=8)
        ./testall.sh --threads --max 4  # in-process pthreads (`t4 -st -sn -N`)

The raytracer is deterministic, so the image it produces must be identical
whatever the arrangement.  pic/rayM.ppm are the reference renders, and
testall.sh compares against them byte for byte - which makes it a real test
of the link fabric, the chain bootstrap and the task farm, not a smoke test.

To run one arrangement by hand, eg. 3 processors:

        export SPYNET=raytrace3.map
        t4 -s8 -sl -sn 1 -si &          # node 1 FIRST - see below
        sleep 1
        t4 -s8 -sl -sn 2 -si &
        t4 -s8 -sl -sn 0 -sb raytrace3.btl

The shell script multi.sh/multi.bat does this for you.


Two rules the obvious version of the above gets wrong
-----------------------------------------------------

1.  Node 1 must be started first, and given time.

    With shared-memory links, node 1 CREATES the shared segment and every
    other node attaches to it.  A node that starts before the segment exists
    does not retry - it exits immediately and silently.  Starting all the
    workers in one loop is a race that kills node 2 as soon as there are
    three or more processors.

2.  A killed run leaves the segment behind, and poisons the next one.

    The segment is SysV shared memory keyed on the map file, and it refuses
    to be created twice ("SHMLinks already exists").  If a run is interrupted
    before it can clean up, the next run of that same size fails at startup.
    Check with `ipcs -m` and remove the leftover with `ipcrm -m <shmid>`;
    testall.sh does this automatically.


Link transports
---------------

-sl selects shared-memory links, which only work between processes on one
machine.  Without -sl the emulator uses ipc:// sockets instead, which need
t4 to have been built with nanomsg available - Makefile.linux detects it via
pkg-config.  If it was not, every inter-node message fails with

        channel_sendP: Failed to send 1 bytes on Link2Out (Invalid argument)

and -sl is the only option.

`t4 -sn -N` is a third way: it makes this process node 0 of an N-node
network and spawns the other N-1 emulators itself.  Convenient interactively,
less so from a script, because the children are not yours to clean up.
